// @check
// @error cannot infer a type for 'none' — an empty array literal has no element type until it is used; use it where its type is known, or annotate the binding (`const none: i32[] = []`)
// @error cannot infer a type for 'm' — an empty Map has no key or value type until it is used; use it where its type is known, or annotate the binding (`const m: {[string]: i32} = Map()`)
// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR D1591, AND THE PIN ON ITS WORDING. Reading `.length` says the
// binding is a list and nothing more; no annotation, no push, no store, no delivery to a typed
// destination — so no line in the program says what the element type is, and the refusal is
// the right answer rather than a capability gap.
//
// The message names BOTH routes out. `add a type annotation` alone read as though annotating
// were the only one, while the route the compiler actually serves everywhere — deliver the
// value where its type is known — is what D1591 wired at the inferred-return positions. The
// suggested line is spelled with the binding's own name and its own constructor so it can be
// pasted.
const none = []
print(none.length)
const m = Map()
print(m.keys().length)
